{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Identify the type of graph (distance-time, speed-time, conversion)",
   "For distance-time: gradient = speed",
   "For speed-time: gradient = acceleration, area = distance",
   "Read values carefully from the axes",
   "A flat section on a distance-time graph means stationary"
  ],
  "title": "How to Work with Real-Life Graphs",
  "content": "<p><strong>Distance-time graphs:</strong> The gradient gives the <strong>speed</strong>. A horizontal section means the object is stationary. A steeper line means faster speed.</p><p><strong>Speed-time graphs:</strong> The gradient gives <strong>acceleration</strong>. The <strong>area under the graph</strong> gives the distance travelled.</p><p><strong>Conversion graphs:</strong> Straight lines that convert between units (e.g. miles ↔ km, £ ↔ €).</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>A car travels 60 km in 1.5 hours, then stops for 30 minutes.</strong></p><p>Speed = 60 ÷ 1.5 = 40 km/h. The graph shows a line from (0,0) to (1.5, 60) then a flat line to (2, 60).</p>"
 },
